ers 008-010 and 012-018 are probably allocated to utilities like ls and du.\n\nI'll just make a reasonable assumption and move forward with the task rather than continuing to speculate. I need to pull in the du PRD from the product-requirements directory (which the use case references), then look at the architecture docs and existing command patterns from wc and cat to understand the structure, plus check the sys and format packages for the specific utilities I'll need for disk usage calculation and human-readable output formatting.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm setting up manual flag parsing that handles both combined short flags like -sh and long flags with -- terminators, supporting all the du-specific options like -a, -s, -c, -h, -k, -d/--max-depth, and --si. Then I'll implement recursive directory traversal using pkg/sys.Lstat to avoid following symlinks, accumulating disk usage as I traverse.\n\nFor output, I need to format each line with size, tab, and path using pkg/format.HumanSize with the appropriate binary setting, and add a grand total line when -c is specified. Error handling should match GNU du's behavior—printing permission denied or nonexistent path errors to stderr with a \"du: \" prefix while continuing to process remaining arguments, exiting 0 on success.\n\nFor the implementation approach, I'm using manual flag parsing over os.Args rather than the flag package to match the project's GNU-compatible conventions, relying on pkg/sys.Lstat instead of the standard library to access the Blocks field from syscall.Stat_t for accurate disk usage across different platforms, and installing a SIGPIPE handler to handle broken pipe scenarios gracefully.\n\nTo prevent double-counting hard-linked files, I'm tracking visited files by their device and inode pair in a nested map structure, which aligns with how GNU du counts each file's blocks only once regardless of how many hard links point to it. The acceptance criteria focus on successful compilation, passing 