{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017xXXKv5XwWfBuLQADeFVSQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies correctness through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (utility implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstraction), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded externally).\n\n**Current state**: From source code, these packages exist and are implemented: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge. The code_status fields showing \"not started\" for these are stale.\n\n**Next release**: rel01.3 (du) is the earliest release with spec_complete status and no existing implementation. rel00.0 through rel01.2 are effectively complete (source code exists for all generation targets). rel02.0+ has pending specs, blocking code generation.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\n- **rel00.0**: 3 of 4 use cases have code (format, sys, testutils). uc001-magefiles targets cmd/version only (per DD7), but this is extremely trivial (~15 lines).\n- **rel01.3**: du — spec_complete, no source code. Substantial utility requiring directory traversal, size accumulation, and integration with pkg/sys and pkg/format. This is the next meaningful generation target.\n- **rel02.0+**: Specs pending — cannot generate code.\n\ndu is the right target: it's the earliest substantial spec-complete use case without implementation, and it exercises pkg/sys (Blocks/Lstat) and pkg/format (HumanSize) — both already implemented.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/du main.go -- directory traversal and disk usage reporting (rel01.3-uc001-du)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml (use case for du, references the PRD by name)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.3.yaml (test suite for du with concrete test cases)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the du PRD referenced from the use case above -- likely prd008-du.yaml or similar)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component, pkg/sys and pkg/format responsibilities, DD6 locale constraint)\n      - cmd/wc/main.go (pattern for manual flag parsing with combined short flags, long flags, output formatting with bufio.Writer)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (pattern for manual flag parsing, processFile structure, error message format)\n      - pkg/sys/stat.go (FileInfo struct -- Blocks field for disk usage, Lstat for symlink-safe metadata)\n      - pkg/format/size.go (HumanSize function and HumanSizeOpts for -h and --si output)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"du implementation: flag parsing, recursive directory traversal, size accumulation, formatted output\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement manual command-line flag parsing for du following the same\n          pattern used in cmd/wc/main.go and cmd/cat/main.go: loop over\n          os.Args[1:], handle combined short flags (e.g., -sh), long flags with\n          -- prefix and = or space-separated values (e.g., --max-depth=3), and\n          the -- flag terminator. Support the flags specified in the du PRD. At\n          minimum support: -a (all entries not just directories), -s (summarize\n          -- display only total for each argument), -c (produce grand total),\n          -h (human-readable 1024-based), -k (1024-byte blocks), -d N /\n          --max-depth=N (limit output depth), --si (1000-based human-readable),\n          -L (dereference symlinks), -P (do not dereference, default), -0\n          (null-terminated output), -b/--apparent-size (use apparent size not\n          disk usage), and --block-size=SIZE.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement recursive directory traversal that accumulates disk usage\n          using pkg/sys.Lstat (or pkg/sys.Stat when -L is active) for file\n          metadata. Calculate disk usage from FileInfo.Blocks * 512 (matching\n          GNU du block-count semantics). Track visited inodes by dev+ino pair\n          to avoid double-counting hard-linked files. Default output unit is\n          1024-byte blocks. When -b/--apparent-size is active, use\n          FileInfo.Size instead of Blocks.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement output formatting matching GNU du: each line is the size\n          value followed by a tab character followed by the path, terminated by\n          newline (or NUL with -0). Use pkg/format.HumanSize with Binary=true\n          for -h and Binary=false for --si. When -c is specified, print a grand\n          total line with label \"total\". Output goes through bufio.Writer\n          flushed before exit. When -s is active, print only the total for each\n          top-level argument (suppress per-file output within directories).\n          When --max-depth is active, suppress output for entries deeper than\n          the specified level.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement error handling matching GNU du: on permission denied,\n          nonexistent path, or other I/O errors, print a diagnostic to stderr\n          with \"du: \" prefix followed by path and system error message, then\n          continue processing remaining arguments. Exit 0 on full success,\n          exit 1 if any error occurred during traversal. Call\n          sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() before any output. Flush bufio.Writer\n          before os.Exit; exit 1 on flush failure.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Use manual flag parsing (loop over os.Args[1:]) matching the pattern\n          in cmd/wc and cmd/cat. Do not use the flag package. This matches the\n          project convention for GNU-compatible flag handling with combined\n          short flags.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use pkg/sys.Lstat (or pkg/sys.Stat with -L) for all file metadata\n          rather than os.Lstat or raw syscall. pkg/sys provides the Blocks\n          field from syscall.Stat_t and handles Darwin/Linux divergence in\n          Stat_t field names (prd002-sys R2.3).\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Track visited files by dev+ino pair to avoid double-counting hard\n          links. Use map[uint64]map[uint64]bool keyed by Dev then Ino. This\n          matches GNU du behavior where each file's blocks are counted once\n          regardless of hard link count.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/du/main.go compiles successfully with \"go build ./cmd/du/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/du/\" reports no issues\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          The implementation uses pkg/sys.Lstat (or pkg/sys.Stat for -L) for\n          file metadata and pkg/format.HumanSize for -h/--si output -- no\n          duplication of syscall wrapping or size formatting logic from pkg/\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          Error messages are written to stderr with \"du: \" prefix including\n          the path and system error, and the program continues processing\n          remaining arguments after each error\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          main() calls sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() before any output,\n          matching the pattern established in cmd/wc and cmd/cat\n      - id: AC6\n        text: |\n          Flag parsing supports combined short flags (-sh means -s -h),\n          long flags with = separator (--max-depth=3), and the -- flag\n          terminator, consistent with cmd/wc and cmd/cat patterns\n      - id: AC7\n        text: |\n          Hard-linked files are counted only once by tracking dev+ino pairs\n          across the traversal\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65799,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65799},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d7d649b-759c-4d9f-b3c4-462466a3b601","uuid":"27779640-62a8-45dd-90fb-7c828e661ba8"}
